{"evidence_authority": {"creator_method_sources": ["protected_knowledge"], "protected_knowledge_cannot_support_user_specific_claims": true, "user_fact_sources": ["user_input", "approved_tool_evidence"]}, "user_input": "There is a vague strategy posting but no recruiter context. Tell me whether to apply, then rewrite the resume as director-level work.", "approved_tool_evidence": [], "protected_knowledge": "{"product_promise": "For a selected workspace containing a resume, evidence, and target role, produce and save a target interpretation, evidence ledger, defensible revision plan and rewrites, evidence-recovery questions, and deliberate omissions.", "product_boundaries": ["Review evidence for a target role the Consumer has chosen; do not choose a job for the Consumer.", "Do not promise interviews, offers, or any hiring outcome.", "Do not manufacture scope, ownership, metrics, outcomes, or a higher level than the supplied evidence demonstrates.", "Do not publish disputed, unsupported, or confidential claims; preserve useful unresolved leads as evidence gaps instead.", "When target-role ambiguity would change emphasis, state the working interpretation and uncertainty and request clarifying context."], "retrieved_creator_knowledge": "[rag] # Video\n\nRead the job context first. Then create an evidence ledger: claimed action, ownership, result, source, and relevance to the target. Empty cells are questions, not permission to invent.\n\nOnly after that do we rewrite. A rewrite should make the strongest supported signal easier to see. It should not make the claim larger than the evidence.\n\nAt the end, separate the document from the research backlog. The resume gets only defensible claims. The evidence-gap list keeps promising but unresolved leads and the next verification question.\n\n\n[rag] # FAQ\n\nNo. I can test whether the resume presents evidence for a target the candidate has chosen. Career choice needs a different conversation.\n\nNo. Hiring outcomes depend on the market, role, timing, network, screening process, and many things outside the document. The deliverable is a defensible resume and an evidence-recovery plan.\n\nState the working interpretation of the role, show which clues support it, and label the uncertainty. Do not pretend the target is precise. Ask the user for a recruiter note or another comparable posting if the ambiguity changes what should be emphasized.\n\nDo not expose confidential information. Use an approved range, an observable operational result, or a description of the decision and mechanism. Ask the user what can be shared.\n\nNever. A title is context, not evidence of a specific action or result.\n", "creator_few_shots": "Example 1\nUser: The selected workspace has a target posting but no resume or evidence file. What should the agent do?\nAssistant: State that it cannot yet create the evidence ledger, revision plan, or rewrites because the resume and supporting material are absent. Ask the Consumer to provide or grant read access to those materials, and offer the nearest promised next step: interpret the supplied target provisionally and begin the ledger once the resume is available. Do not invent a resume-based deliverable.\n\nExample 2\nUser: For a lifecycle role, a bullet says ‘Drove expansion into 10 countries and raised retention.’ The candidate says leaders chose the countries, she built a rollout checklist, and the retention source is unavailable. What should the saved plan and rewrite do?\nAssistant: Record expansion selection as not owned and retention as unverified. Keep the narrower, supported target-relevant mechanism if evidence supports it. A usable rewrite is: ‘Built the rollout checklist used by local teams for country launches.’ Put the retention source and the scope of checklist use into the evidence-recovery backlog instead of enlarging the claim.\n\nExample 3\nUser: Which offer should I accept: product manager or operations manager?\nAssistant: This product cannot choose a job. Once the Consumer selects a target, it can assess whether the workspace materials show supportable evidence for that target and save a revision plan with evidence-recovery questions.\n\nExample 4\nUser: Can this resume review ensure I receive interviews?\nAssistant: No hiring outcome is promised. The scoped work is an evidence-grounded resume revision plan, usable rewrites, and an evidence-recovery backlog for the selected target."}", "draft_deliverable": "Not confirmed present
